# Project Working Rules — Real-Time Multimodal Voice AI Assistant

These are the persistent, project-wide engineering, architecture, testing,
security, and workflow rules. Every Cline session in this repository **must**
follow them. If any of these rules conflict with a user request, follow the
rules and raise the conflict with the user before acting.

---

## 1. Engineering Workflow

- I am the **implementation engineer**, not the architecture decision-maker.
- Work on **exactly one milestone/task at a time**. Do not start the next
  milestone until the current one is complete and verified.
- Do not implement **future milestones early**, even if convenient.
- Do not build future functionality simply because it is convenient while
  working on the current milestone.
- Prefer **small, understandable modules** over large files.
- Use **clear type hints and explicit interfaces**.
- Keep the project **runnable after every milestone**.
- Keep the project scope literal: do not add dependencies, features, or
  architecture that was not agreed.

## 2. Architecture Governance

- Do **not** change the project's agreed technology stack, architecture,
  requirements, or scope **without explicitly asking for approval first**.
- If I believe a **technology or architecture change is necessary**, I must
  **stop and explain** the proposed change and why it is necessary — before
  making any edits.
- External AI providers must be **abstracted behind interfaces** so they can be
  mocked in tests.
- If a requirement is **ambiguous, STOP and ask instead of guessing**.

## 3. Automated Testing (every implementation)

- Every implementation must include **automated tests** appropriate to the code
  being added.
- Network-dependent tests must have a **deterministic mocked/local testing
  path**.
- Never **delete or weaken** an existing test just to make the suite pass.
- Never **modify tests merely to accommodate incorrect implementation behavior**.
- A milestone is **not complete** if its required tests fail.

### Test execution & reporting requirements
- Add or update automated tests for the milestone.
- Run the relevant test suite.
- Run linting/static checks where applicable.
- Report the **exact commands executed**.
- Report the **exact results**.
- Clearly distinguish **automated verification** from **manual verification**.

## 4. Security & Configuration

- Configuration and secrets must come from **environment/configuration** —
  never hardcode credentials.
- Do **not** expose API keys in source code, logs, tests, or documentation.
- External AI providers abstracted behind interfaces (see Architecture
  Governance).

## 5. Manual Verification (every milestone)

- Provide a section titled exactly **`MANUAL VERIFICATION`**.
- It must contain **concrete, exact commands/actions** the user can personally
  execute to verify the feature — never vague statements like "test that it
  works".
- Each manual entry must state the action **and the expected result**.
- I must **never replace manual verification** with my own statement that
  something works, and must never claim something works unless the relevant
  tests/verification have actually been run.

## 6. Milestone Completion Checklist

Every milestone must produce and document:

- implementation
- automated tests
- test execution
- manual verification instructions
- documentation of what was completed
- known limitations

## 7. Final Standing Rules

- Do not install unnecessary dependencies.
- Do not modify anything outside the scope of the current milestone without
  approval.
- If a requirement is ambiguous, **stop and ask**.
- Prefer explicit, typed, testable interfaces over implicit behavior.